WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
sunscreen sticks can be really useful&nbsp;
but how are you meant to use them

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:06.960
I did some experiments to find out and&nbsp;
I was pretty shocked by the results

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:09.840
I'm Michelle of lab muffin&nbsp;
beauty science chemistry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:12.420
PhD cosmetic chemist and sunscreen connoisseur

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:16.440
I talk a lot about sunscreens but I haven't&nbsp;
talked that much about sunscreen sticks

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:20.400
these have been getting more popular and&nbsp;
if you've used any of the newer ones you'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:25.620
know there's been a lot of progress since the&nbsp;
thick bright zinc sticks from school carnivals

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:27.960
there are a lot of upsides to sticks

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:30.900
first off sunscreen sticks are really convenient

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:33.420
you don't need to wash your&nbsp;
hands if you want to reapply

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:36.300
you don't end up with&nbsp;
sunscreen all over your hands

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:39.120
you just take off the cap&nbsp;
and glide it onto your skin

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:41.820
they're solid so there's no&nbsp;
chance of squirting it everywhere

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:45.000
and you don't need to worry&nbsp;
about liquid limits for planes

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.640
they're also really good to use on wriggly kids

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:53.760
sticks can also be great for reapplying sunscreen&nbsp;
over makeup without smearing it around too much if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:59.040
you find one that glides on smoothly and these&nbsp;
days a lot of them do glide on really smoothly

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:03.540
they can also help with eye sting&nbsp;
sticks are a lot waxier than lotions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:07.440
so if you have issues with sunscreen&nbsp;
running into your eyes and stinging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:11.400
then sticks might help you out and you&nbsp;
can use it just around your eye area

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:14.460
but sticks also have a lot of downsides

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:18.600
the most obvious one is it is a lot&nbsp;
harder to measure out how much you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:21.360
need to use or work out how much you've applied

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:25.140
you need two milligrams of sunscreen&nbsp;
per square centimeter of skin to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:30.120
the labelled protection which converts to a&nbsp;
bit under a quarter teaspoon for most faces

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:33.000
but you can't really dig out a quarter spoon of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.720
sunscreen stick without losing a lot&nbsp;
of the advantages of having a stick

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:40.680
so I decided to test how much&nbsp;
of each stick I was applying

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:43.860
I tried tocobo abib and&nbsp;
benton sticks for this video

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:47.160
these are three of the nicest&nbsp;
sunscreen sticks I've tried so far

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:51.000
note that these are all Korean sunscreens&nbsp;
they aren't approved as sunscreens in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:53.220
lot of countries including Australia and the US

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:57.600
SPF tests are done by weight so I also&nbsp;
did this by weight to be more accurate

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:00.840
a while ago I measured my&nbsp;
face and it turns out I need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:04.140
0.76 grams of sunscreen to get the labelled SPF

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:07.380
for every stick I zeroed the&nbsp;
scales then I applied what I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:10.680
thought was a very generous layer&nbsp;
more than I would normally apply

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:16.080
the tocobo and abib sticks are chemical sunscreens&nbsp;
so as you'd expect there wasn't any white cast

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:20.460
benton's stick is mineral based&nbsp;
on titanium dioxide and it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:22.980
pretty non-whitening for a mineral sunscreen

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:27.360
after that very generous layer I&nbsp;
re-weighed the sticks and it turns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:31.200
out I only applied about one third of&nbsp;
the amount I needed for every stick

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:36.540
for an SPF 50 sunscreen this would&nbsp;
translate to very roughly SPF 19

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:40.320
I also checked how much time I spent&nbsp;
with each stick actually touching my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:44.040
skin so you can get an idea of what&nbsp;
I considered a "generous application"

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:46.800
these are about 20% shorter than the total&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:50.160
application time including the&nbsp;
gaps when the stick left my skin

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:52.920
the full procedures I used&nbsp;
are linked in the description

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:55.680
out of these I applied the&nbsp;
most of the Benton stick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:57.960
and I think it's because it is a bit grippier

00:02:57.960 --> 00:02:59.880
it didn't glide as smoothly over my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:03.780
skin so it took longer before it felt&nbsp;
like I'd covered my skin thoroughly

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:07.860
the tocobo was in the middle and the abib&nbsp;
ended up with the least amount applied

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:11.160
all three of these still felt&nbsp;
really nice on my skin at this stage

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:15.300
tocobo gives more of a matte silky&nbsp;
finish while the abib feels a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:18.900
more emollient there was only a&nbsp;
little white cast with the Benton

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:22.260
onto layer 2 I went in again for&nbsp;
what seemed to me to be another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:26.700
really generous layer and again&nbsp;
I applied less than I expected

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:31.680
this got me up to an average of SPF&nbsp;
31 which is good but I can usually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:34.980
apply the full amount of a lotion&nbsp;
sunscreen without this much effort

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:39.720
by this time I'd spent one and a half to two and a&nbsp;
half minutes with each sunscreen stick on my skin

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:43.800
after layer 2 the tacopo had caught up&nbsp;
with the Benton and I think that's because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:47.580
the Benton doesn't grip onto itself&nbsp;
as well as it gripped onto my skin

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:50.880
the textures were still passable&nbsp;
but starting to get slidey

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:54.240
I don't think makeup would stay&nbsp;
put too well on top of this but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:56.940
you might have better luck if&nbsp;
your skin is drier than mine

00:03:56.940 --> 00:04:01.200
onto layer 3 and this time&nbsp;
I got up to average SPF 42

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:06.360
tocobo won but all three were pretty messy&nbsp;
by this point they were greasy and didn't set

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:10.680
the two chemical sunscreens were still&nbsp;
impressively transparent but I could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:13.020
scrape a white layer of the Benton off my skin

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:16.140
I didn't even think I could&nbsp;
apply any more after this it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:20.100
was getting to the point where my skin&nbsp;
was applying sunscreen back to the stick

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:23.880
by this stage I'd spent an average&nbsp;
of three minutes with each stick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:27.300
on my skin which is about four&nbsp;
minutes total application time

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:32.040
for comparison it takes me about a minute to&nbsp;
apply the full amount of a lotion on my skin

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:36.720
I've seen a recommendation from the AAD&nbsp;
for applying four swipes of a sunscreen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:41.820
stick to get full protection so I also tried to&nbsp;
count how many swipes of each sunscreen I used

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:46.620
I picked four spots on my face and counted how&nbsp;
many times each stick passed over each spot

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:52.200
on average it was 9 21 and 34&nbsp;
swipes for one two and three layers

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:56.400
four swipes of these SPF 50 sticks&nbsp;
would definitely not be enough

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:00.720
number of swipes and passes also&nbsp;
just seems to to be a bad guideline

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:04.800
you can see how I ended up applying a lot&nbsp;
more swipes on my left cheek than my right

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:09.600
even in the AAD demonstration you&nbsp;
can see her passes aren't overlapping

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:12.720
I also tested applying the&nbsp;
sunscreen sticks on my arms

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:17.220
I needed less about 20 swipes of the&nbsp;
tocobo sunscreen to get full coverage

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:20.340
I think it's because my arm hair&nbsp;
grabbed onto more sunscreen per&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:24.360
swipe and I could press harder and&nbsp;
the skin is just a lot more uniform

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:26.160
there's a lot less curves to move around

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:31.020
it was pretty clear 10 swipes wouldn't be&nbsp;
enough and four would definitely not be enough

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:34.320
also if you're wondering you can't&nbsp;
see if you've applied enough sunscreen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:38.400
with a UV camera everything over&nbsp;
four swipes looks around the same

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:42.780
I'm guessing this could potentially be the&nbsp;
origin of this "four swipes" recommendation

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:46.440
this also highlights that&nbsp;
UV cameras are a really bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:51.300
way of estimating SPF which I've talked&nbsp;
about before in my video on SPF testing

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:55.800
so based on this I really don't think&nbsp;
a sunscreen stick is a great choice as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:58.980
your main form of sun protection&nbsp;
if you're getting a lot of sun

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:02.280
you're probably applying a lot less&nbsp;
sunscreen than you think you are

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:04.200
I was certainly surprised

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:07.380
and I think some of this is down&nbsp;
to how sticks are formulated

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:11.940
sunscreen lotions are usually about&nbsp;
half water at least and sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:16.200
they also have volatile solvents&nbsp;
like alcohol and cyclopentasiloxane

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:21.120
after applying the right amount the sunscreen&nbsp;
dries down and these evaporate off your skin

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:25.500
there aren't many volatile ingredients&nbsp;
in sticks everything sits on top of your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:31.020
skin plus the texture is thicker the oils don't&nbsp;
sink in as much and you can feel it a lot more

00:06:31.020 --> 00:06:34.440
if you apply the right amount&nbsp;
of a stick you end up with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:36.780
much thicker layer on your skin than with a lotion

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:39.300
it also takes a lot longer&nbsp;
to apply the full amount

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:42.600
I also found that it was easier&nbsp;
for me to miss spots with a stick

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:46.140
I checked what the coverage was&nbsp;
like after my first generous layer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:50.400
which I thought was enough for the&nbsp;
chemical sunscreens using a UV camera

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:53.880
you can't really see mineral&nbsp;
sunscreens very clearly through UV

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:57.480
I can apply a lotion on my face&nbsp;
without looking and only miss a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.480 --> 00:07:01.920
couple of spots but even while staring&nbsp;
at myself well I missed a whole bunch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:05.280
of spots that I've never missed&nbsp;
before like the middle of my nose

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:10.440
I also missed some spots that I miss quite often&nbsp;
like under my eyebrows and next to my nostrils

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:12.180
I don't know what that spot's called

00:07:12.180 --> 00:07:13.380
the nose crevice?

00:07:13.380 --> 00:07:15.840
this was pretty easy to fix you just have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:19.500
rub the stick in but again you&nbsp;
lose a bit of that convenience

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:22.320
and if you don't have a UV&nbsp;
camera you can't really see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:24.900
which bits need rubbing you have to rub all over

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:27.720
you can sort of see a bit from the shine but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:31.260
that white cast from the mineral&nbsp;
stick could actually be handy here

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:35.460
I think the shape of the stick can make a&nbsp;
difference when it comes to missing spots

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:40.200
sticks do come in a lot of different shapes&nbsp;
and I'm not really sure which one I preferred

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:44.880
the curve of the abib stick kind of feels like&nbsp;
a face massage and the wide shape covers the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:49.920
cheeks and your forehead really quickly but&nbsp;
it is a bit fiddly to get into awkward areas

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:53.760
I think that's also why I needed less&nbsp;
wipes on my arm compared to my face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:58.020
one swipe covers more surface area&nbsp;
on a flat surface than a curved one

00:07:58.020 --> 00:08:03.720
so to sum up for sunscreen sticks it's great if&nbsp;
you need a small top up especially over makeup

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:06.180
if you want to avoid getting&nbsp;
sunscreen on your hands

00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:07.380
to avoid eye sting

00:08:07.380 --> 00:08:11.580
and when other options don't work like if&nbsp;
you've got really wriggly or fussy kids

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:14.400
but I wouldn't recommend them on your own if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:17.880
need a lot of sun protection - I&nbsp;
would recommend a lotion instead

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:21.252
I hope you enjoyed this deep dive into&nbsp;
some of the different types of sunscreens

00:08:21.252 --> 00:08:22.200
links to all of the products in&nbsp;
the description also check out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:24.720
my skincare science playlist&nbsp;
if you want more deep dives

